<edge source="global_financial_turmoil::sovereign_credit_spreads" target="israel_palestine_flashpo::diplomatic_reciprocity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Widening sovereign credit spreads constrain fiscal space and political capital for regional governments and external donors, reducing their willingness or capacity to undertake robust reciprocal diplomatic measures (e.g., sanctions escalations, mission changes) in disputes over settlements.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_turmoil</data>
  <data key="d39">israel_palestine_flashpo</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_financial_turmoil::sovereign_credit_spreads" target="russia_ukraine_war::negotiation_willingness" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Widening sovereign credit spreads for a belligerent or a principal donor (e.g., Ukraine or key backers) creates acute fiscal financing pressure and urgency to unlock external support, increasing that party's willingness to make concessions and enter negotiations.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_financial_turmoil</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
